<!-- SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS -->
<section class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-number">6</div>
    <h2>Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  </div>

  <ol class="findings-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Crude-to-retail transmission is near-total at the global level</strong>
      <p>Weekly Brent&ndash;petrol correlation of r=1.00 means consumers in low-subsidy countries bear the full burden of commodity volatility. Retail prices rose 78% over the period, matching Brent exactly. Policymakers in these countries have limited natural insulation from global price shocks without active intervention.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Subsidies are the dominant price-setting mechanism, dwarfing all other factors</strong>
      <p>Very-high-subsidy countries deliver retail petrol at $0.15/L vs. $3.45/L in low-subsidy countries &mdash; a 95.7% reduction. This effect is stable across years and crude price levels. However, with Brent now sustained above $120/bbl, the fiscal cost of maintaining these subsidies is proportionally higher than during the 2020 trough.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Very-high subsidies successfully decouple retail from crude, but at high fiscal cost</strong>
      <p>The pass-through ratio for very-high-subsidy countries is 0.324, meaning only one-third of Brent price movements reach consumers. Medium-subsidy countries show near-complete pass-through (0.99), suggesting that moderate intervention provides minimal price buffering.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The regional price gap is widening, not converging</strong>
      <p>The Europe&ndash;Middle East spread grew from $1.61/L (2020) to $2.87/L (2026), a 78% increase. This divergence implies that existing policy regimes are amplifying rather than reducing global fuel price inequality across regions.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Low-income countries face the same price volatility as high-income countries</strong>
      <p>All three income tiers show a coefficient of variation of ~17.8%. While absolute prices are lower in low-income countries ($1.39/L vs. $3.55/L), proportional instability is identical &mdash; and these countries have the least fiscal capacity to implement stabilization mechanisms.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Taxation is progressive across income levels but regressive within them</strong>
      <p>High-income countries tax fuel at 34.7% vs. 21&ndash;23% in middle/low-income countries. This creates a global progressive structure. However, within any given country, fuel taxation is inherently regressive as lower-income households spend a larger share of income on fuel.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Medium-subsidy regimes offer the weakest policy return</strong>
      <p>With a pass-through ratio of 0.99 (virtually complete), medium-subsidy countries incur fiscal expenditure on subsidies while providing almost no consumer price protection. Policymakers in these 18 countries should either deepen subsidy commitments or redirect spending to targeted cash transfers.</p>
    </li>
  </ol>

  <div class="finding-box blue">
    <strong>Policy Recommendation Summary</strong>
    <p><strong>For high-subsidy producers:</strong> Maintain current subsidy architecture but establish fiscal stress-testing against sustained $130+/bbl scenarios. Begin gradual transition to targeted subsidies (means-tested) to preserve fiscal sustainability.<br>
    <strong>For medium-subsidy countries:</strong> Current spending yields negligible consumer benefit (pass-through &asymp; 1.0). Redirect subsidy budgets toward direct income support or strategic petroleum reserves.<br>
    <strong>For low-subsidy, high-tax countries:</strong> Consider implementing automatic stabilizer mechanisms (variable tax rates that flex with crude prices) to smooth retail price volatility during supply shocks, as practiced by some European jurisdictions during the 2022 crisis.<br>
    <strong>For low-income countries:</strong> Prioritize building strategic buffer funds during low-price periods. The identical volatility exposure (CV=17.8%) combined with limited fiscal capacity makes these economies most vulnerable to the next supply shock.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- DATA LIMITATIONS -->
<section class="section" id="limitations">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-number">&bull;</div>
    <h2>Data Limitations &amp; Methodology Notes</h2>
  </div>
  <ul style="padding-left:20px; font-size:14px; color:var(--color-text-secondary);">
    <li>Tax percentage shows high within-country weekly variability (e.g., US ranges from 14.1% to 65.0%), which may reflect estimation methodology rather than actual policy changes. Averages should be interpreted with caution.</li>
    <li>Subsidy classification is binary/categorical (four levels) and static across the period, meaning countries that changed subsidy policy mid-period are not captured.</li>
    <li>2026 data covers only Q1 (through April 6), making year-over-year comparisons for 2026 partial.</li>
    <li>All prices are in USD, meaning exchange rate fluctuations are embedded in the retail price changes observed for non-USD economies.</li>
    <li>Brent Crude is uniform across countries for a given week, serving as a benchmark rather than the actual crude procurement cost for any specific country.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

